{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqgu63huoyl","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Codex apply_patch carries target paths in patch body not tool_input file_path","summary":"The orchestrator write gate (hooks/orch-gate.sh + .js) extracted the edit target from tool_input.file_path, which Codex's apply_patch tool does NOT populate — the file path(s) live inside the patch envelope. So a CLAIMED Codex worker's apply_patch was never confine-checked and could write outside its attempt worktree undetected (the zero-tolerance no-claim block still fired; this was a confinement leak between claimed workers). Fix: when tool_name is apply_patch (or file_path is empty but a patch payload is present), pull the patch text via a multi-key fallback (tool_input.input ?? patch ?? patch_text ?? diff ?? content ?? text, else a changes[] join, else any string field matching the patch markers) and parse the per-file headers `*** Add File:` / `*** Update File:` / `*** Delete File:` plus the rename `*** Move to:` to get EVERY target path; run each through the same worktree-confinement check as file_path (any path outside the worktree blocks). apply_patch header paths are often RELATIVE to the worker cwd (== the claim worktree), so non-absolute targets are resolved against the worktree before the ancestor test. The actual Codex field name is undocumented in-repo, hence the defensive multi-key fallback mirroring packages/opencode-plugin/lib/gate.js:45. NOTE: the .sh core is the POSIX track and does raw bash `case` prefix-matching without slash-normalizing the daemon worktree path, so its in-worktree/relative confinement can't reconcile separators on win32 (the daemon returns BACKSLASH worktree paths there) — Claude runs the .js on Windows, which handles separators via hookkit cmp/slash; the .sh runs on Codex/Cursor/CI (POSIX).","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T16:08:02.610Z","vec":[-0.11054156720638275,0.013419666327536106,-0.03168630599975586,0.001501112594269216,-0.00674052909016609